          Water-based polyurethane is typically dry enough to recoat in 2 to 4 hours, takes light sock-foot traffic after about 24 hours, and reaches full cure in roughly 7 days to 2 to 4 weeks depending on the product. Oil-based polyurethane is much slower: plan on 8 to 24 hours between coats, a day or two before careful walking, and about 30 days before it is fully cured. Those figures assume room temperature around 70°F and moderate humidity, and the label on your can always wins over any general chart, including ours. The rest of this page explains why the four stages differ, what slows them down, and how to tell when your finish is actually ready.
        </p>

        <div id="four-clocks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What's the Difference Between Dry and Cured?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Polyurethane dries in two separate processes, and confusing them is the root of most finishing problems. First, the carrier evaporates. In water-based poly that is mostly water plus a little co-solvent; in oil-based poly it is mineral spirits or a similar solvent. Once enough has gone, the film stops being wet and sticky. That is what labels mean by dry to the touch, and later, dry enough to recoat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second process is curing, where the resin itself hardens. Oil-based poly cures by reacting with oxygen in the air, which crosslinks the resin into a tough film over days and weeks. Water-based poly cures as tiny resin particles fuse together and, in many products, crosslink further. Either way, the film keeps getting harder long after it feels dry, and it is only at full cure that it reaches its rated scratch, chemical and water resistance.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gives you four practical milestones. Dry to the touch means dust will not stick and a light fingertip leaves no mark. Recoat time means the film is firm enough to sand or overcoat without wrinkling or lifting. Light use means careful walking in socks or setting light objects down. Full cure means normal life: furniture, rugs, pets, cleaning products and a dropped set of keys.
          </p>

          <p>
            The gap between the first and last milestone is the part people underestimate. A floor that feels perfectly hard on day two can still dent under a chair leg, mark under a rubber-backed rug or cloud under a wet mop, because the film has not finished hardening. Treat the first week, and with oil-based poly the first month, as a protected period even if the surface looks done.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
