        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Water-based polyurethane is the quick one: recoat in two to four hours, walk on it in socks the next day, and expect full cure somewhere between a week and a month depending on the product. Oil-based is the slow one: 8 to 24 hours between coats and about 30 days to cure. Temperature, humidity and coat thickness shift every one of those numbers, and the can's label is the final word.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The habits that matter most are simple. Apply thin coats, wait for the sanding test to pass before recoating, ventilate the room rather than blasting the surface, and protect the finish well past the point it feels dry. Rushing a floor saves a day and can cost the whole job.
          </p>
